# Deliberately narrow. There is no `* text=auto` line, and that is a decision, not an
# omission: a blanket rule would renormalize hundreds of tracked files in one
# unreviewable commit, while other Claude Code sessions may be working in the same
# directory (see CLAUDE.md).
#
# What this line does, stated accurately -- because the reason first written here was
# wrong, and so was the one in .github/workflows/ci.yml:
#
# The repository ALREADY stores every Go file with LF. Measured, not assumed: all 64
# were extracted with `git show :<path>` and none contained a CR, and `gofmt -l` over
# that content flagged nothing. A Linux CI runner therefore already saw clean gofmt
# output, and the CRLF that shows up locally on Windows is `core.autocrlf=true`
# translating on checkout -- a working-tree artifact, never a repository state.
#
# So this line fixes nothing that was broken. It PREVENTS a future break: a contributor
# with `core.autocrlf=false` on Windows would otherwise commit CRLF, and gofmt treats CR
# as content, so that single commit would turn the gofmt gate red for reasons unrelated
# to code quality. `eol=lf` makes the storage convention explicit instead of relying on
# every contributor's local git configuration.
#
# The gate itself was never actually blocked by line endings. ci.yml said it was; that
# claim was never tested and is false. It is added in this same change, and ci.yml's
# comment is corrected rather than left standing.
*.go text eol=lf
